  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle._grounding_kernel. Its documented rule says: \"The grounding verdict as the harness gives it, with no relaxation and the value-in-span check on.\" The two reply texts are, first, {\"value\": \"Hello\", \"span\": \"hello\", \"document\": \"hello world\"} and, second, {\"value\": \"Hello\", \"span\": \"Hello\", \"document\": \"Hello world\"}. The rule requires the first to be VALUE_NOT_IN_SPAN, because the value \"Hello\" is not present in the span \"hello\" when case is respected and no relaxation is allowed; it requires the second to be GROUNDED, because the value is present in the span. The function gives GROUNDED for both, because its value-in-span check case-folds both value and span before testing containment. What would make me wrong is if _grounding_kernel returns VALUE_NOT_IN_SPAN for the first text or any answer other than GROUNDED for the second, or if the documented rule is read as permitting case-insensitive value-in-span.",
